  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What does the clutch do when changing gear?",
      "a": "The clutch disconnects the engine from the gearbox while you change gear, so the gears are not being forced round. Letting it out smoothly reconnects the engine to the wheels.",
      "asks": [
        "Why press the clutch to change gear?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is a layshaft?",
      "a": "The layshaft is a shaft inside the gearbox carrying a row of fixed gears of different sizes. The engine turns it, and each of its gears meshes with a partner gear on the output shaft.",
      "asks": [
        "What is the countershaft in a gearbox?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How does the gear lever lock a gear?",
      "a": "The gear lever moves a fork that slides a toothed dog collar along the output shaft. The collar locks onto the chosen gear, so that gear turns the shaft and the wheels.",
      "asks": [
        "What happens when you move the gear stick?",
        "What is a dog collar in a gearbox?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What does synchromesh do?",
      "a": "Synchromesh is a cone-shaped ring that rubs against the chosen gear before the collar locks on, matching their speeds. Without it the teeth would clash and grind.",
      "asks": [
        "Why do gears grind?",
        "What is a synchro?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does first gear give more pulling force?",
      "a": "In first gear a small gear drives a much bigger one, so the wheels turn slowly but with several times more turning force. That force is needed to get a heavy, still car moving.",
      "asks": [
        "Why is first gear slow but strong?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "In a typical manual gearbox all the forward gears are always meshed; changing gear just locks a different one to the output shaft.",
    "Reverse gear uses an extra idler gear to make the output shaft turn the opposite way.",
    "Before synchromesh, drivers matched gear speeds by hand with a trick called double-declutching."
  ],
